      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Most forensic linguistics research suggests these patterns are more linked to social identity and presentation rather than memory accuracy. Juries often exhibit \"linguistic bias,\" where deviations from standard dialects are subconsciously associated with lower competence or trustworthiness. While syntax doesn't prove a memory is false, it can activate implicit stereotypes related to the speaker's background. Thus, students must learn to differentiate real evidentiary flaws from accent or register-based prejudicial judgment.",
